course: dictionary
lesson: 6947
title: Painted: past, picture and paint
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Painted is the past form of paint. Paint is what you do now, paints is what someone does now, and painted is what was done before. «Yesterday she painted the fence blue» means the painting is already finished.
explain: Painted can also describe a thing. «The painted fence» is a fence that has paint on it, so painted tells you about the fence. Ask yourself: is this word an action that has already happened, or a word that describes something?
explain: Watch for capital letters. «Painted Jaguar lives there too» has a capital P at the start, so it is a character's name, not the past form of paint. A capital letter in the middle of a sentence is a clue that you are reading a name.
fact: Painted is the past form of paint: «She painted a horse» means she has already made the picture.
fact: Painted can describe a thing covered in paint, like a painted door or painted nails.
fact: With has or had, painted is used again: «I have painted my room» means the job is done.
fact: In science, the painted lady butterfly is named for the patterns that look painted on its wings.
fact: A capital letter at the start of a word can show it is a name, like «Painted Jaguar» in a story.
exercise: «Yesterday Mia painted her bedroom wall green.» What does painted show? | Something happening right now | Something that will happen tomorrow | A girl's name | *Something already done
exercise: «The painted fence looked bright in the sun.» Which word describes the fence? | looked | *painted | sun | fence
exercise: «Painted Jaguar lives there too.» Why is Painted a name here? | It is the past form of paint | It is a number | It means covered in colour | *It starts with a capital letter and names a character
test: Mum says, «I painted the kitchen door last week.» What does painted mean? | Still wet and happening now | A kind of food | Something that will be painted tomorrow | *Colour was put on it, and it is finished
test: Which sentence uses painted as a describing word? | She painted a horse yesterday | They have painted the wall | I will paint the gate tomorrow | *The painted lady butterfly flew past
exam: «She ___ a picture of the sea last summer.» Which word fits the gap? | painting | paint | *painted | paints
